1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,654
Do photos of receipts actually count?

2
00:00:02,954 --> 00:00:04,175
Yes. If they’re good.

3
00:00:04,625 --> 00:00:07,494
IRS guidance says the rules for paper records

4
00:00:07,494 --> 00:00:09,330
also apply to electronic ones.

5
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:11,755
They just have to be legible,

6
00:00:11,755 --> 00:00:13,611
complete, and easy to find.

7
00:00:14,061 --> 00:00:16,080
So a good photo shows the whole receipt,

8
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:17,955
from the store name to the total.

9
00:00:18,255 --> 00:00:20,350
The date, items and amount are sharp.

10
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:21,570
And it’s backed up,

11
00:00:21,570 --> 00:00:22,772
not just on your phone.

12
00:00:23,222 --> 00:00:24,245
Here’s the twist.

13
00:00:24,245 --> 00:00:26,625
The photo often outlasts the paper.

14
00:00:26,625 --> 00:00:28,280
Most receipts are thermal paper,

15
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:29,339
and they fade.

16
00:00:29,789 --> 00:00:31,345
So snap it the day you get it,

17
00:00:31,345 --> 00:00:32,809
while the print is still sharp.

18
00:00:33,309 --> 00:00:35,265
Find us at receipt-nest.com,

19
00:00:35,265 --> 00:00:37,008
on the App Store and Google Play.
